  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul class=\"marker:text-textOff list-disc\">\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\"><strong>Normal Range:</strong>&nbsp;Mercury levels within reference range typically indicate no recent or significant mercury exposure.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 9px;\">\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\"><strong>Elevated Mercury (&gt;9 ng/mL):</strong>&nbsp;Suggests recent or ongoing exposure to mercury. Clinical correlation recommended. May indicate exposure to methylmercury (common from seafood) or inorganic/metallic mercury depending on exposure history.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 9px;\">\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\">Serum mercury reflects primarily methylmercury exposure, common with dietary intake.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 9px;\">\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\">Levels should be interpreted with consideration of timing of exposure, type of mercury, and clinical symptoms.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 9px;\">\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\">Blood levels can decrease rapidly as mercury redistributes to tissues; early sample collection post-exposure is critical.</span></p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
